preConditionHook:
// The pullback in progress has lasted no more than maxPullbackBars bars. A
// brief pullback is Brooks's sign of strength; NEGATED with the per-placement
// toggle it is his sign of weakness ('the pullback extends for several
// bars'), which is how the fourth source states the same test at four bars.
//
// THE ANCHOR IS THE SETTLED LEG COUNTER'S RESET (Ranges ch17, R17-01): the
// pullback starts at the last bar whose close made a fresh resetLookback-bar
// high, measured against EL's Highest(high, resetLookback)[1] -- the N bars
// ENDING ONE BAR AGO -- so the bar's own high cannot defeat its own test.
// That is the design decision which lets this rule leave research grade: the
// sources say 'the pullback' without saying when it started, and the
// fresh-N-bar-high reset supplies a concrete start with no pivot search.
// Extremes() is EasyLanguage source, so the scan carries the relational
// tolerance through el_gt / el_lt rather than raw > and <.
//
// The counter is a plain bars-since with no window indexing, so nothing here
// reaches deeper than resetLookback however long the pullback runs. On the
// anchor bar itself the count is 0 and the rule passes. Before the first
// anchor of the run the count simply grows from its initial 0, in both twins
// alike, so the rule does not pass spuriously during warm-up.
//
// MIRRORED, not identical: the merged slice recorded sides = same, but the
// anchor is directional -- a bull pullback is measured from a fresh N-bar
// high and a bear pullback from a fresh N-bar low.
priorHigh = high[1];
priorLow = low[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)resetLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], priorHigh)) priorHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], priorLow)) priorLow = low[i];
}
if (el_gt(close[0], priorHigh)) {
    barsSinceHigh = 0;
} else {
    barsSinceHigh = barsSinceHigh + 1;
}
if (el_lt(close[0], priorLow)) {
    barsSinceLow = 0;
} else {
    barsSinceLow = barsSinceLow + 1;
}
